<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Betzi'as HaPas — Halachah 5<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">Part 4: §§ 41–49 — Conclusion</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Conclusion of <span class="v">"L'Hamshich Shalom"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM I, 14</span>)</span></div>

<!-- §41 -->
<div class="sn">§ 41 — Why We Read the Story of Yosef on <i>Chanukah</i></div>

<div class="p">We read the Torah portions about Yosef during <span class="h">Chanukah</span> because on <span class="h">Chanukah</span> we must draw clean garments through the <span class="h">Menorah</span>'s oil = the <span class="h">da'as</span> of Torah: <span class="v">"at all times let your garments be white, and oil upon your head never be lacking."</span> This = the inauguration of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where Torah is fixed. All of this is accomplished through the power of the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> = Yosef HaTzadik. Therefore we read about him on <span class="h">Chanukah</span>.</div>

<!-- §42 -->
<div class="sn">§ 42 — Ten Fingers on the Bread</div>

<div class="p">When blessing <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>, one places both hands — ten fingers — on the bread, corresponding to the ten <span class="h">mitzvos</span> connected to bread. The essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> and <span class="h">birur</span> of eating is through Torah. The ten fingers also correspond to the Ten Utterances of Creation and the Ten Commandments = the totality of Torah.</div>

<!-- §43 -->
<div class="sn">§ 43 — The Honor of Food: <i>Kavod</i> and the Table</div>

<div class="p">It is forbidden to treat food disrespectfully — especially bread. The essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> of eating = raising <span class="h">kavod</span> from the degradation of exile. Therefore food must not be degraded. Accordingly, when one wishes to honor another, one typically offers food and drink — for there the essential <span class="h">kavod</span> resides: <span class="mx">"the table of kings — the King of Glory."</span></div>

<div class="p">This is what Yosef said: <span class="v">"tell my father of all my honor [<span class="h">kevodi</span>] in Mitzrayim"</span> — <span class="mx"><span class="h">kevodi</span></span> specifically: he merited to raise the holy <span class="h">kavod</span> in Mitzrayim. Therefore: <span class="v">"hurry and bring my father down here"</span> — Yaakov need not fear the contamination of Mitzrayim overwhelming his children, for Yosef has already raised the <span class="h">kavod</span> there. Through his power, Israel can endure until the redemption. The entire exile = the <span class="h">kavod</span> of holiness falling among the nations. Yosef's message: <span class="mx">"even now I have merited to raise the <span class="h">kavod</span> — through this power they can endure until the <span class="h">kavod</span> returns to Israel in fullness."</span></div>

<!-- §44 -->
<div class="sn">§ 44 — Fixed Place for Meals: Perseverance and the <i>Bais HaMikdash</i></div>

<div class="p">When one establishes a fixed place for eating, one person exempts another with the blessings of <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> and <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span>. Without a fixed place — eating scattered — one does not exempt another (<span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span>, <span class="h">siman</span> 167).</div>

<div class="p">As explained: all <span class="h">birur</span>-stages correspond to the clarification of bread. The essential <span class="h">birur</span> — the hardest — is at the end, in the eating-clarification. One must be enormously strong, an enormous stubborn one, because the obstacles intensify at every stage — stemming from soiled garments that demand immense effort to strip. The suffering and confusion change form constantly with every person and every moment — one must strengthen oneself anew each time.</div>

<div class="p">This would be impossible without Hashem's mercy: He gave us the Torah through Moshe Rabbainu and sent awesome <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who made wondrous <span class="h">tikkunim</span>, <span class="kc">establishing a fixed place for Torah</span>. This is Dovid/<span class="h">Mashiach</span> who labored all his life until he found a place for Hashem = the place of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> = the place of the <span class="h">Aron</span>. Because the obstacles are overcome through Torah, Torah must be established with enormous fixedness — so that at every moment one can flee to Torah in whatever befalls him. The fixedness of the <span class="h">bais midrash</span> / the <span class="h">yeshivah</span> of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> = <span class="h">mikdash me'at</span>: a fixed place for Torah so that everyone can flee there at all times, and the Torah's fixedness will stand against all obstacles.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Words of the wise are like goads, and like nails well-planted — [given] by masters of assemblies [<span class="h">ba'alei asufos</span>]"</span> (Koheles 12). The Sages: <span class="mx">"Just as a goad directs the cow to her furrow, so words of Torah direct a person"</span> — one must be guided and steered constantly, for each time he veers, the Torah redirects him. <span class="mx">"Just as a nail is fixed, so words of Torah are fixed"</span> — Torah must be anchored with enormous permanence. <span class="v">"Masters of assemblies"</span> = the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who gather and unify souls, arousing root-souls through Torah — birthing converts, stirring sinners to <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, strengthening everyone constantly by drawing new illumination from root-souls each time. <span class="v">"Given by one Shepherd"</span> = the great <span class="h">shalom</span> drawn through all this, as explained in <span class="lr">LM I:14</span>. Rashi: <span class="mx">"even though some declare pure and some impure, some permit and some forbid… all were given by one Shepherd"</span> = the great peace made through Torah-engagement that arouses root-souls.</div>

<!-- §45 -->
<div class="sn">§ 45 — Why a Fixed Place for Eating Enables One to Exempt Another</div>

<div class="p">When a fixed place is established for eating, one person can exempt another with <span class="h">berachos</span>. Through the fixed place, one receives the power of the eating-<span class="h">birur</span> from the fixed place of Torah — which derives from the fixed place of the <span class="h">Aron</span> that Dovid found, where the eating-<span class="h">birur</span> reaches its perfection: <span class="v">"you shall eat there before Hashem your G-d."</span> All eating everywhere draws its <span class="h">birur</span>-power from there = <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> blessing <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>.</div>

<div class="p">One person exempts another because there — at the fixed place of Torah — souls are included in one another through the Torah-study of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who arouse root-souls = <span class="h">ba'alei asufos</span>. This = the fixed place of the <span class="h">Aron</span>, where all root-souls are encompassed: <span class="v">"its interior is lined with love."</span> Because the souls are included in one another, one can exempt another. Without a fixed place, souls are scattered and cannot be included — and one cannot exempt another.</div>

<div class="sep">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

<div class="p" style="font-style:italic;color:var(--section-num);text-align:center;">The following sub-sections (§§46-49) are listed in the source under<br>"Devarim HaNohagin B'Seudah" but are a direct continuation of this halachah.</div>

<!-- §46 -->
<div class="sn">§ 46 — Changing Location During a Meal</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">halachah</span> (<span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span>, <span class="h">siman</span> 178): foods requiring a <span class="h">berachah acharonah</span> in the place where they were eaten — if one changes location, one need not bless again (as the Rema rules following Rav Chisda: <span class="mx">"what is the reason? — to his first place he returns"</span>).</div>

<div class="p">The essential perfection of a person = being so fixed in Torah and service that wherever he goes — through all the changes of location in the world — he always returns to his fixed place = the fixedness of Torah, where the essential rest and permanence lie. Everyone knows: a person has no rest in this world; he is constantly unsettled and confused even at home: <span class="v">"by day it does not rest, by night it does not lie down."</span> The only rest and permanence = Torah and service: <span class="v">"for you have not yet come to the rest and the inheritance."</span></div>

<div class="p">One must strive to connect with true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who can establish him in Torah so firmly that wherever he is — physically or spiritually — he will never be cast off completely, but will always return to Torah. <span class="v">"It goes to the south and circles to the north — and to its place it yearns, there it shines"</span> — wherever one goes and circles, one yearns toward one's place = Torah's fixedness. <span class="v">"If the spirit of the ruler rises against you — do not leave your place."</span></div>

<div class="p">This = the fixed place of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, concerning which it is written: <span class="v">"for you have not yet come to the rest and the inheritance… and it shall be the place that Hashem your G-d chooses"</span>: <span class="v">"this is My rest forever."</span> This = the fixedness of <span class="h">batei midrash</span>: drawing one's mind always to the fixedness of Torah. <span class="v">"In Hashem I take refuge — how can you say to my soul: flee your mountain like a bird?"</span> (Rashi explains: regarding exile from <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>.) Dovid says: since I take refuge in Hashem, always directing my desire toward Torah's fixedness = Har HaMoriah where the <span class="h">Aron</span> rests — how can you say my soul wanders? I am never a wanderer from Torah. <span class="v">"Built as <span class="h">Talpios</span>"</span> — <span class="mx">"a hill [<span class="h">tel</span>] that all mouths [<span class="h">pios</span>] face toward"</span> = all desires are anchored there — the ultimate strengthening: wherever one is, one faces toward the fixed place of Torah.</div>

<!-- §47 -->
<div class="sn">§ 47 — <i>Yisro</i>: The Convert Who Heard and Came</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Yisro heard"</span> — <span class="mx">"What report did he hear that made him come? The splitting of the sea and the war with <span class="h">Amalek</span>."</span> <span class="h">Parshas Yisro</span> is juxtaposed to <span class="h">Parshas B'Shalach</span>, which speaks of the splitting of the sea and concludes with the war against <span class="h">Amalek</span>: <span class="v">"Hashem shall have war with <span class="h">Amalek</span> from generation to generation."</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Amalek</span> = the essence of the serpent's contamination = the totality of all obstacles and barriers from the soiled garments. <span class="h">Amalek</span> lurks against every Jew at every moment of approach toward holiness, intensifying each time anew. Therefore Hashem Himself must fight him: <span class="v">"war for Hashem against <span class="h">Amalek</span>, from generation to generation."</span> His primary assault is against converts and <span class="h">ba'alei teshuvah</span>, upon whom the obstacles from soiled garments are most intense.</div>

<div class="p">The defeat of <span class="h">Amalek</span> comes through <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span> [splitting of the sea] — which was the essential completion of the Exodus and the beginning of the journey to <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. At the sea, Pharaoh and his army fell completely; Israel left his domain and sang: <span class="v">"bring them and plant them on the mountain of Your inheritance."</span> The splitting was through Yosef's coffin: <span class="v">"the sea saw and fled"</span> — the Sages: <span class="mx">"it saw the coffin of Yosef"</span> who had <span class="v">"fled outside."</span> This = the power of the holy graves of the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who achieved the ultimate perfection of guarding the <span class="h">bris</span>. Caleiv, when the <span class="h">meraglim</span>'s slander intensified against <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, went and prostrated himself on the graves of the Avos — and through this merited what he merited: <span class="v">"only the land upon which he trod…"</span></div>

<div class="p">Yisro — the true convert — understood all this. He heard that although the war against <span class="h">Amalek</span> is enormous and relentless (= the soiled garments intensifying constantly), Hashem has already prepared the remedy in advance: <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span> through Yosef's coffin. Through the power of the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s grave, the raging waters were parted and the springs of wisdom opened: <span class="mx">"a maidservant at the sea saw what Yechezkel and Yeshayah never saw."</span> With this power, <span class="h">Amalek</span> can be defeated at all times. Yisro heard, understood — and converted.</div>

<!-- §48 -->
<div class="sn">§ 48 — The Final Defeat of <i>Amalek</i>: Rest and Inheritance</div>

<div class="p">The complete annihilation of <span class="h">Amalek</span> will only come when Israel reaches the rest and inheritance: <span class="v">"when Hashem your G-d gives you rest… you shall erase the memory of <span class="h">Amalek</span>."</span> As long as the holy rest is not fully achieved, <span class="h">Amalek</span> intensifies each time. But he is defeated each time through Torah and the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — especially the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who dwell in the dust, who have already arrived at perfect rest: <span class="v">"peace shall come; they shall rest upon their beds, the upright walker"</span> (Yeshayah 57); <span class="v">"there the weary of strength shall rest"</span> (Iyov 3); <span class="v">"go to the end and rest"</span> (Daniel 12).</div>

<div class="p">This is what Na'ami said to Rus when she wished to convert: <span class="v">"shall I not seek rest [<span class="h">mano'ach</span>] for you, that it may go well with you?"</span> — <span class="mx"><span class="h">mano'ach</span></span> [rest] specifically. Rus merited, through the greatness of her true conversion, to strip the soiled garments completely and clothe herself in <span class="h">Shabbos</span> garments = rest. From her descended <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, who will bring Israel to the rest and inheritance in body and soul, speedily in our days, <span class="h">amen</span>.</div>

<!-- §49 -->
<div class="sn">§ 49 — The Angels' Meal at Avraham's Tent</div>

<div class="p">When the angels came to Avraham, he gave them to eat. Through this he demonstrated to them that they should not accuse Israel for wanting to receive the Torah — even though they are destined to sin. The Midrash: <span class="mx">"when Moshe ascended to receive the Torah, the angels sought to strike him… [Hashem said:] 'Is this not the one at whose table you ate?'"</span></div>

<div class="p">When the angels visited Avraham, the Torah's revelation was beginning to dawn: Avraham had just been circumcised (the first <span class="h">mitzvah</span> commanded to him), and they came to announce Yitzchak's birth — from whom all Israel would descend to receive the Torah. Therefore Avraham endeavored to protect Israel from future accusation. He gave the angels <span class="kc">food to eat</span> — so they would know and understand the immensity of what a human being must endure in this world: being forced to eat, which involves such clarifications that even inside the body the good must be separated from literal refuse. <span class="kc">Therefore one cannot accuse a person if he stumbles</span> — for he is in places so degraded that he must clarify from literal filth.</div>

<div class="p">Indeed, though the angels ate in great holiness as befits angels — <span class="mx">"the first course was cleared and the next brought"</span> — they did not emerge unscathed. The Sages: they were forced to remain in this world and could not ascend for some time. One angel could not return until the days of Yaakov; another not until the days of Mano'ach. For <span class="kc">from this world it is exceedingly difficult to emerge clean</span>. A descending angel cannot return to his place until he immerses many times in the River of Fire [<span class="h">Nahar Dinur</span>]. The essential clarification of good from contamination in this world = eating. Because Adam's sin was through eating, the contamination clings most intensely there.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Avraham honored the angels specifically with food — showing them the immensity of the suffering and bitterness a person endures. They can no longer accuse Israel for not receiving the Torah: <span class="kc">it is absolutely necessary to give us the Torah, for without it, it is impossible to survive this world.</span> Torah = holy eating: <span class="v">"were it not that Your Torah is my delight, I would have perished."</span></div>

<div class="p">When the angel came to Mano'ach and Mano'ach wished to honor him with food, the angel refused: <span class="v">"if you detain me, I will not eat of your bread."</span> He no longer wished to eat — his time to ascend had arrived, and he did not want to be delayed again, having already tasted the heaviness of the eating-<span class="h">birur</span> at Avraham's table. <span class="v">"Working wondrously [<span class="h">mafli la'asos</span>]"</span> was said of his ascent — for ascending from this world's contamination is a great wonder. <span class="kc">This is why the Sages established in <span class="h">Birchas Asher Yatzar</span>: <span class="v">"<span class="h">u'mafli la'asos</span>"</span> ["who acts wondrously"]</span> — because this clarification, where the good separates from the refuse inside the body, is a great and wondrous marvel.</div>

<div class="sep">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary — Part 4 (§§ 41–49) ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§41</strong>: We read about Yosef on <em>Chanukah</em> because clean garments are drawn through the <em>Menorah</em>'s oil through the power of the <em>tzadik</em> = Yosef.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§42-43 — Ten fingers, honor of food</strong>: Ten fingers on bread = ten <em>mitzvos</em> = Ten Utterances = Ten Commandments = Torah. Food must be honored because the eating-<em>tikkun</em> = raising <em>kavod</em>. Yosef: "tell my father of all my <em>kavod</em> in Mitzrayim."</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§44-45 — Fixed place for meals</strong>: One exempts another only with a fixed place — because fixedness draws <em>birur</em>-power from the fixed place of Torah / the <em>Aron</em>. There, souls are included in one another through <em>ba'alei asufos</em>. <em>Ba'alei asufos</em> = <em>tzadikim</em> who gather and unify souls. "Words of the wise are like goads… and nails well-planted" = Torah must be anchored permanently.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§46 — Changing location</strong>: Wherever one goes — always return to Torah's fixedness. "Built as <em>Talpios</em>" = all desires face toward the fixed place of Torah. This is the <em>Bais HaMikdash</em>'s essence.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§47-48 — Yisro, <em>Amalek</em>, and Rus</strong>: <em>Amalek</em> = the serpent's contamination = all obstacles from soiled garments. Defeated through the <em>tzadik</em>'s grave (= <em>Krias Yam Suf</em> through Yosef's coffin). Yisro heard this and converted. Complete annihilation of <em>Amalek</em> only when Israel reaches the "rest" = <em>Mashiach</em>, who descends from Rus the convert who merited <em>mano'ach</em> [rest].</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§49 — The angels' meal</strong>: Avraham gave the angels food to show them the immensity of human suffering — so they could not accuse Israel for wanting the Torah. Even the angels could not emerge clean from eating in this world. The angel at Mano'ach refused to eat a second time. <strong><em>U'mafli la'asos</em></strong> in <em>Asher Yatzar</em> = the wonder of the eating-<em>birur</em> inside the body.</p>
</div>

